WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.300
Do you use Twitter, Facebook, YouTube, Instagram, or Snapchat to keep up with the world?

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.700
68% of Americans get at least some of their news through social media,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.780
and for the under-30 set that figure is a lot higher.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.480
The tech giants are private companies,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.640
and for the most part have the freedom to determine what's posted on their platforms.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:24.660
But are their choices about what content to remove, and who to evict really being made free from government pressures?

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.960
It's not enough to just give people a voice.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.280
We need to make sure that people aren't using it to harm other people or to spread misinformation.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.820
Can you define hate speech?

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.420
Senator I think that this is a really hard question.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.680
We should be particularly worried about

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.000
asking platforms to take things down if they're over in this realm

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.800
where it's very hard to tell what's illegal,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.460
and where what you're taking down might be important public political speech.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.260
Daphne Keller is a former Google attorney who's currently a law professor at Stanford.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.120
She says that the tech giants are facing pressure from governments worldwide

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.580
to clamp down on a variety of content on their platform.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.000
From terrorist propaganda to disinformation to hate speech.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The European Union is currently debating new so-called Terrorist Content Regulations,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.620
which would mandate that tech giant's prohibit so-called violent or extremist speech and propaganda  on their platforms.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:25.020
Keller says the worst versions would empower police to inform tech companies

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:31.340
when they see posts violating the terms of service social media users agree to when first signing up.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.420
The European Commission has looked to terms of service as

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.580
a means of getting platforms to take down content

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.700
without going through the process of passing a new law or a regulation.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.480
The hate speech code of conduct was negotiated between

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.420
Microsoft, YouTube, Facebook, Twitter, and the European Commission, and that

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.420
commits those platforms to using their terms of service to prohibit hate speech

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.380
under a definition that's derived from European law.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.840
But because they're using their terms of service to do it

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.299
that means that the prohibition is global.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.020
So are we in a sense living under

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.680
European Union internet regulation right now in America?

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.360
To a certain extent.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.060
Things that would be politically difficult and perhaps unconstitutional,

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.840
or in some cases certainly unconstitutional, to mandate here

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:30.400
we don't have to even consider because they get mandated in Europe and then companies apply them globally.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.380
Twitter CEO Jack Dorsey says that his company follows a speech code developed by the United Nations.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.640
Doesn't the UN declaration guarantee the right of all people through any medium to express their opinion?

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.760
It does—and why do they have hate speech—it does, and it also has conditions

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.360
around particular speech inciting violence, and

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.860
some of the, some of the aspects that we speak to as well.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.020
And it protects specific categories.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:56.040
Whether it's religion, race, gender, sexual orientation,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.720
those are all also protected under the UN covenant.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.840
Article 20 of the United Nations Covenant on Civil and Political Rights that Dorsey references

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.380
says that so-called hate speech, which it defines as

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.080
any advocacy of national, racial, or religious hatred that

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.600
constitutes incitement to discrimination, hostility, or violence

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.040
shall be prohibited by law.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.436
The First Amendment by contrast prevents the US government

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.000
from regulating speech based on content.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.400
People might be airing legitimate grievances

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.397
about something their government is doing,

00:03:28.397 --> 00:03:30.680
or something that a foreign government is doing.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.739
They might be engaging in conversation

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.840
that is very important in their political context.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.300
If that gets mistaken for terrorism

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.060
by a sort of trigger-happy employee sitting in California,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.120
that's going to disrupt really core political speech.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.900
Keller calls this effect "collateral censorship",

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.960
and says we've seen it on the internet before

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.920
when strict copyright enforcement led to unnecessary takedowns.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.040
We've found that there are a lot of bad faith notices

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.860
you get things like the government of Ecuador

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.540
using a nonsense copyright claim

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.400
to get journalistic criticism taken down.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.520
The Church of Scientology used some spurious copyright accusations to get criticism taken down,

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:21.360
and there's research showing that some platforms just don't even bother to try to decide

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.140
which allegations are true and which allegations are false.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.460
They just honor all of them.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:29.480
I am optimistic that over a five to ten year period

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.120
we will have AI tools that can

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.380
get into some of the nuances, the linguistic nuances,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.720
of different types of content to be more accurate and flagging things for our systems.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.560
Does that solve the problem?

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:41.480
No.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.940
AI does not solve the problem.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:49.640
Everybody who claims that there are filters, or AI, or machine learning

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.567
that can solve these problems,

00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:53.840
has something to sell.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.460
The public has very little information about how well any of it is working.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.380
There are two major studies I'm aware of

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.560
about the efficacy of filters in identifying unlawful content,

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.920
and both of them cast serious doubt.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.820
One study out of Reggie University Amsterdam found that AI designed to catch hate speech on Twitter

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.511
misclassified at 80% of the time.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:23.820
Another joint study out of China and the UK found that hate speech "lacks unique discriminative features"

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.000
and is "difficult to discover."

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.420
And after Europe reached an agreement with the tech companies to build a terrorism hash database

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:36.400
that used a AI to automatically flag and remove extremist propaganda,

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.840
YouTube took down an entire archive meant to document war crimes in Syria.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.280
These lawmakers and policy makers are motivated by

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.460
wanting to stop the spread of terrorism,

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:50.660
wanting to stop what's become known as self-radicalization.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:58.160
What's the big deal that a few things get taken down accidentally if we're stopping people from joining ISIS?

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.760
Right now we don't have any clear information telling us that

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:07.480
taking down all of these videos really is making us safer.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.980
In fact, a lot of people who are expert researchers on security are

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:18.660
concerned that this will effectively drive more people into darker corners of the internet,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:26.080
into echo chambers where they only ever hear from people who agree with a violent agenda.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.680
There are people in law enforcement who would prefer sometimes to leave things up

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:36.440
so that they can monitor what's going on and go after the the perpetrators of real-world violence.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:41.280
So it's not clear what the security upside is.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.400
At the same time,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.420
it's not entirely clear what the speech and expression down side is.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.180
We know there's a downside,

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.540
we know those 100,000 videos from the Syrian archive

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:56.120
that were documenting violence in Syria for future prosecution disappeared.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.020
We know there's harm to speech,

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.860
but we can't quantify that either,

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.180
because we don't have the data and transparency

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.000
from platforms or from governments to understand that side of the calculation.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.140
Back in the US,

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:11.300
Democratic presidential candidate Elizabeth Warren has publicly called

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.780
for the breakup of the tech giants.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.480
And the Federal Trade Commission and Justice Department

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.840
are gearing up for antitrust investigations into Facebook and Google.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:25.381
And conservatives like Republican Senators Josh Hawley and Ted Cruz

00:07:25.381 --> 00:07:29.500
have called for social media companies to be treated more like public utilities

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.920
that have to treat all content as neutral, or lose their liability protection.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.800
Right now big tech enjoys an immunity from liability

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.821
on the assumption that they would be neutral and fair.

00:07:39.821 --> 00:07:42.920
If they're not going to be neutral and fair, if they're gonna be biased

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:47.200
we should repeal the immunity from liability so they should be liable like the rest of us.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.940
I don't even know what being neutral would mean.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:55.680
Would it mean allowing every single thing to be uploaded and just showing it in chronological order?

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.560
Can you be neutral if you have search features?

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.520
Can you be neutral if you allow people to mute things?

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:10.760
A truly neutral, meaning showing everything that is legal, social media platform

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:14.800
would be full of content that most users don't want to see.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:18.660
And that certainly advertisers don't want to have next to their ads.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:23.020
Keller says that the best way to reduce the power of Google, Facebook, and Twitter

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:27.040
is through new competition which regulation can stifle.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.060
So when Mark Zuckerberg requests a more active role for governments

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:33.300
in monitoring his own company

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.920
perhaps his underlying motive is to protect Facebook from competition

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.160
by raising the barriers to entry.

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:43.360
There does seem to be the sense that Twitter and Facebook

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:48.340
are controlling the conversation in a way at this point, the public conversation.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:53.288
Do you think that is going to continue to be the case going forward,

00:08:53.288 --> 00:08:56.740
or are we on the verge of something different?

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.760
If we're on the verge of something different, I don't know what it is.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:06.500
And I think if we continue seeing laws passed that more or less entrench incumbents,

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:11.560
by making requirements that only they can afford to comply with,

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:16.840
that makes it much less likely that we ever get to this other future

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:20.160
where we get away from the the current "information gatekeepers"

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.220
having the role that they do now.

